{"characterisation":[{"file":"tests/acceptance.part1.test.tsx","name":"CHAR_OverviewEmptyStateShowsApprovedWords","target":"S-1 fixture empty state keeps its approved title and hint"},{"file":"tests/acceptance.part1.test.tsx","name":"CHAR_ProcurementErrorStateShowsApprovedWords","target":"S-2 fixture error state keeps its approved message"},{"file":"tests/acceptance.part1.test.tsx","name":"CHAR_FeedEmptyStateShowsApprovedWords","target":"S-3 fixture empty state keeps its approved title and hint"}],"notes":"I wrote all 16 tests in tests/acceptance.part1.test.tsx. I ran the file once. The 13 acceptance tests fail on an assertion: the stub containers still show the design's sample data, so none of my own values appear on the page. The 3 characterisation tests pass. `npx tsc --noEmit` reports no errors in the test file; the only errors are in the generated lib/api/client.msw.ts. I did not run the tests a second time.\n\nThe API is answered by a fetch stand-in that records every call, so each test sets its own responses and can assert on the request body. I used values that differ from the contract's examples and the design's sample data, so a screen showing sample data cannot pass.\n\nThree guesses to check when the screens are built:\n- **Layout:** the tests find a record's values by walking up from its name (PO number, lot name or item name) to the nearest block that holds all of them and none of the other records' names. This assumes each record sits in its own row or card.\n- **Form labels:** the mapping dialog and the \"Add inventory item\" form have no approved wording. The tests find their fields by label patterns such as /lot/i, /pen/i, /head|animals/i, /name/i, /category/i, /available/i, /unit of measure/i, /cost/i, /dry matter/i, /energy/i, /storage|location/i and /opening/i. The inventory form is opened through \"Feed tools\" and then \"Add inventory item\". Rename the labels or adjust the patterns if the form differs.\n- **Sync request:** AC-2.1 says the request carries PO line, vendor, cost and the other fields. The contract's SyncRequest only has source, includePurchaseOrders and includeReceiving. I assert exactly that request body (source \"mock\", both flags true). I assert the PO line, vendor, cost, head count, weight, breed, age and source on the page from the response.\n\nAC-3.1 has two tests, one for a single lot and one for a split across two lots.\nAll 15 tests are in tests/acceptance.part2.test.tsx. They render the S-3 container with a recording fetch stand-in that answers /api/feed, /api/yard and the POST endpoints. The stand-in returns data in the contract's shape, with values chosen so a screen that only echoed what the user typed would fail. The file compiles (`npx tsc --noEmit` reports nothing for it). I ran it twice, and all 15 fail today as expected. The failures are assertions or \"element not found\" on the current fixture-only screen, not set-up crashes.\n\nWhat I had to guess:\n- **Form and label names.** The plan, record-actual and new-ration forms use the approved wording (\"Pen\", \"Saved plan\", \"Delivered quantity (lb)\", \"Calculate and save ration\" and so on). The stock-movement form has no approved wording, so AC-9.1 finds its fields by loose label patterns (/ingredient|item/, /quantity/).\n- **Split-button menus.** The helpers try the \"Feeding entry: more\" and \"Feed tools: more\" menu items first. If those are absent, they click the main button and use whatever form opens.\n- **Horizon selection (AC-10.1, AC-10.2).** The tests click a tab, radio, button or \"horizon\" select named after each horizon if one exists. If none exists, they assert the values are visible without selecting.\n- **Event detail (AC-16.x).** The tests assert inside the dialog if one opens. Otherwise they assert inside the event's table row, found by its pen name and a unique cost.\n- **\"Lowest cost of gain\" marker (AC-12.1).** The tests accept the word \"lowest\", \"best\" or \"cheapest\" on the right ration's card, and fall back to a page-wide match only when the card is just the name.\n- **Currency (AC-13.1).** The test uses EUR and accepts \"€\" or \"EUR\" next to the per-lb price, and rejects a \"$\" price.\n\nNumbers are matched with optional thousands commas, and variance is matched with a minus sign. No test reads the real clock: the plan date comes from TEST_NOW (tomorrow).\nAll tests are in tests/acceptance.part3.test.tsx and use msw with the contract's generated handlers, overridden with my own rows. I ran the file once; all 12 fail on assertions or missing labels and rows against the fixture-only containers, and none crashes in set-up. `npx tsc --noEmit` reports errors only in generated lib/api/client.msw.ts, none in this file. The containers are still stubs, so the tests guess at the built UI: lot select (native or custom dropdown), a plain date input for \"Measurement date\", the edit sheet's save button (matched by /save|revis|update/), the void control in the row or its \"More\" menu with an optional confirm dialog, and \"Adjust future plan\" as a button or a menu item under \"More\". AC-19.1 accepts ascending or descending time order. AC-22.3 can only prove the screen shows the returned feed-cost-per-gain value, because the yard response carries no non-feed costs. AC-23.1 is covered by the weight save on S-2 only, because S-3 has no approved field labels to drive its feeding save. AC-20.1 has three tests (weight edit, weight void, feeding void). No HTTP probes and no characterisation tests, as instructed.\nAll 11 tests are in tests/acceptance.part4.test.tsx. I ran the file once after a small timeout fix, then once more to read the failures. Every test fails on an assertion. None crashes in set-up or rendering, and none passes. They fail now because the containers still render the static design fixtures and never call the API.\n\nEach test answers the API with msw handlers started from the contract's generated handlers. I override the answers with my own rows: three lots, pens, report rows and presentation sections that differ from each other. The report handler filters its rows by the query it receives (lotId, breed, sex, penId) and echoes the filters.\n\nHow each group checks its criterion:\n- **AC-24.1, AC-28.1, AC-29.1 on S-1:** assert values inside each lot or pen row, plus the summary numbers. The best lot's row must say \"best\" and the worst lot's row \"worst\" or \"lowest\". The middle lot's row says neither.\n- **AC-25.1:** checks tokens from every returned card: highest-consuming pen, best feed-to-gain lot, highest-ADG lot, most-used ration, plan alignment with days cover, the under- and overfed pens, and cost of gain. It also checks one report row.\n- **AC-26.1 and AC-26.2:** pick filters through the form (Breed, Sex, Pen and Lot, then \"Apply filters\"). They assert the rows returned for that scope with their ADG, FCR and cost-of-gain values. Lots outside the scope must disappear. The selected filter values must still show, and the request must carry them as query parameters. AC-26.2 changes filters twice in a row.\n- **AC-27.1 and AC-27.2:** open \"Export report\" and choose CSV or PDF. They assert that a request or download to export.csv or export.pdf carries the selected scope: breed, sex, penId, lotId, and valid startDate and endDate. The other format must not be requested. The success toast is checked word for word.\n- **AC-29.1:** besides S-1, it has an S-2 test (procurement, lot, pen and weight records) and an S-3 test (inventory, rations, plans and events). I could only cover all seven record types by testing those screens too, even though they aren't in the approved-screens list. Those two tests check record names and numbers from the API data.\n- **AC-29.2:** the accordion titles must appear. Then each section is opened (if it has a button) and its body, which I set to distinct text, must appear.\n\nThings I could not test:\n- The date-range filter is not driven by UI, because the date picker's interaction is unknown. The date range is only checked as valid startDate and endDate values in the report and export requests.\n- A CSV or PDF's contents depend on the server, so those tests assert only the export request, including its scope.\n- Number formatting is not fixed by the design beyond the sample fixtures. Page text is normalised by removing thousands separators, so \"1,913\" counts as \"1913\". My values use the same decimal places as the design fixtures.\n\ntsc shows only errors in the generated lib/api/client.msw.ts. None come from my file.","probes":[],"tests":[{"acId":"AC-1.1","file":"tests/acceptance.part1.test.tsx","name":"AC_1_1_ShowsSharedWorkspaceWithoutSignInOrRoleSelector","screen":"S-1"},{"acId":"AC-1.2","file":"tests/acceptance.part1.test.tsx","name":"AC_1_2_ShowsSameControlsInTwoSessions","screen":"S-1"},{"acId":"AC-2.1","file":"tests/acceptance.part1.test.tsx","name":"AC_2_1_SyncRequestsMockSourceAndShowsPoLineVendorCostHeadWeightBreedAgeSource","screen":"S-2"},{"acId":"AC-2.2","file":"tests/acceptance.part1.test.tsx","name":"AC_2_2_ShowsReturnedVendorCostHeadWeightBreedAgeAndSourceForEachRecord","screen":"S-2"},{"acId":"AC-3.1","file":"tests/acceptance.part1.test.tsx","name":"AC_3_1_ShowsPoLineToLotToPenAssociationWithPenCapacityAndCount","screen":"S-2"},{"acId":"AC-3.1","file":"tests/acceptance.part1.test.tsx","name":"AC_3_1_ShowsEachAssociationWhenPoLineIsSplitAcrossLots","screen":"S-2"},{"acId":"AC-4.1","file":"tests/acceptance.part1.test.tsx","name":"AC_4_1_ShowsEachAffectedRecordWithStatusExistingMappingAndReviewAction","screen":"S-2"},{"acId":"AC-5.1","file":"tests/acceptance.part1.test.tsx","name":"AC_5_1_ShowsReturnedTargetWeightAndProjectedDate","screen":"S-2"},{"acId":"AC-5.2","file":"tests/acceptance.part1.test.tsx","name":"AC_5_2_LeavesProjectedDateEmptyWhenAdgIsZeroOrNegative","screen":"S-2"},{"acId":"AC-6.1","file":"tests/acceptance.part1.test.tsx","name":"AC_6_1_ShowsTargetWeightSelectedForLotSpecificProcurementOrYardTar